      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-452",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We note that $6x^2$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}(2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2x^3+3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{(2x^3+3)^7}{7}+C.\n$$"
    },
