        ":question/problem": "$\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We note that $2x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{13}}{13}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C.\n$$"
