        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-2390",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We note that $10x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=5{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=10x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,10x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=5{x}^{2}-4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$"
      },
